TAM-PANE - A WAYLAND WINDOWING SYSTEM FOR TAMLINUX

tam-pane is our own Wayland compositor and window manager. It is built
fresh, from the ground up, and it builds on the best ideas and the best
code of MangoWM, Hyprland, sway and every other window manager we can
find. Where another project's code works well, we take it and refactor it
into tam-pane's own shape. Where it doesn't, we leave it out. tam-pane is
not a fork or a clone of any of them.

Fred is developing tam-pane while running Tamlinux 0.6.x, with the goal of
dogfooding it as the Tamlinux 0.7.x compositor in place of Hyprland.

Follows the tam-idol 1.1.0 standard.


WHAT IT DOES

  Dynamic tiling with four layouts sharing one interface (0.2.5):
  dwindle (binary split tree), master-stack, scroller (niri-style
  horizontal columns), and monocle, remembered per tag, plus a floating
  scratchpad that stays reachable from any tag.

  Tags on windows, like MangoWM and dwm. A window carries one or more of
  nine tags; a monitor shows every window whose tags meet the set it is
  viewing. One window can appear in several views, and one view can show
  several tags at once.

  Lua scripting built in, like Hyprland. Configuration, key bindings,
  event hooks, window rules and live reload are loaded from
  ~/.config/tam-pane/init.lua using the Lua 5.5 interpreter compiled
  statically into tam-pane (0.1.0–0.1.4, hardened in 0.1.5–0.1.8 after
  review R1). Autostart goes in a startup hook.

  Line-based IPC over a same-UID Unix socket
  ($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tam-pane/<socket>.sock, exported as TAM_PANE_SOCKET)
  with a built-in `tam-pane msg` client for queries, dispatchers, live
  reloads and event subscriptions (0.2.3; see arch/ipc.md).


HOW IT IS BUILT

  - Small modules with one job each, and explicit state: every function is
    handed the server it works on, and there are no global variables.
  - Pure logic (tags, the layout tree) has no compositor code in it, so it
    is tested without a display.
  - The event loop never blocks: no waiting on child processes, and Lua
    runs with an instruction budget, a wall-clock deadline (which also
    covers pattern matching), a memory cap and no blocking calls.
  - Every dependency has to earn its place and has a planned exit. The goal
    is libc, the kernel's headers, libtam and code in this repository, with
    Mesa allowed so the GPU does the compositing.
  - Plain Make. There is no other build system.

  arch/principles.md has the full set of principles.


BUILDING

  Needs: a C17 compiler, make, pkg-config, wlroots 0.20, wayland,
  wayland-protocols, libxkbcommon, and libtam installed under
  LIBTAM_PREFIX (default $HOME/.local).

    make              build build/tam-pane (zero-xcb, XWAYLAND=0 default)
    make XWAYLAND=1   opt-in XWayland build (adds temporary xcb, xcb-icccm)
    make test         unit tests, then headless start/stop checks
    make install      install to PREFIX (default $HOME/.local)
    make uninstall    remove the installed binary
    make clean        remove build/

  The headless checks never touch the running desktop. Running tam-pane
  for real (nested in a window, or as a session) starts with 0.0.2; see
  ROADMAP.md.


DEFAULT KEYS (CUSTOMIZABLE IN INIT.LUA)

  Meta+Return           open a terminal ($TERMINAL, else foot)
  Meta+W                close the focused window
  Meta+J / Meta+K       focus the next / previous window
  Meta+Shift+Arrow      swap the focused tiled window in that direction
  Meta+Ctrl+Arrow       move the focused tiled window in that direction
  Meta+Alt+Arrow        resize the focused split or floating window
  Meta+Left-drag        move a floating window
  Meta+Right-drag       resize a floating window or tiled split
  Meta+V                toggle floating
  Meta+F                toggle fullscreen
  Meta+Space            cycle layout (dwindle, master, scroller, monocle)
  Meta+Shift+Space      cycle layout backwards
  Meta+-                toggle the scratchpad
  Meta+Shift+-          send focused window to/from the scratchpad
  Meta+1 .. Meta+9      view one tag
  Meta+Shift+1 .. 9     move the focused window to a tag
  Meta+Ctrl+1 .. 9      add or remove a tag from the view
  Meta+Shift+Ctrl+1 .. 9 add or remove a tag from the focused window
  Meta+Tab              go back to the previous view
  Meta+, / Meta+.       focus the previous / next monitor
  Meta+Shift+, / .      move the focused window to the previous / next monitor
  Meta+Shift+R          reload ~/.config/tam-pane/init.lua in place
  Meta+Shift+E          quit tam-pane

  Meta is the key with the logo on it.


KEYS NO CONFIG CAN REMOVE

  Handled in C before Lua and before any window sees them (0.1.9):

  Ctrl+Alt+F1 .. F12        switch to that virtual terminal (only when
                            tam-pane runs on the hardware, not nested)
  Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Backspace  emergency quit, even if init.lua removed
                            every binding (gated while session is locked,
                            0.2.0)


WHERE THINGS ARE

  src/, include/   the compositor (one module per file)
  third_party/lua/ vendored Lua 5.5.1 C sources, one marked patch
                   (UPSTREAM.md)
  tests/           unit tests and headless checks
  protocols/       Wayland protocol files we carry
  ideas/           ideas we collect from other window managers
  arch/            architecture: data structures, algorithms, evaluations
  plans/           one plan per work phase (P1, P2, ...)
  bugs/            one record per bug (B1, B2, ...)
  reviews/         one record per review (R1, R2, ...)
  work/            WHATNOW (read it first: what is next) and one record
                   per work session (W1, W2, ...)
  sandbox/         demos and ways for Fred to play with tam-pane
  tools/           helper scripts for developing tam-pane
  ROADMAP.md       milestones with their version numbers
  UPSTREAM.md      every piece of code we took from another project
  AI-BLAME.md      how AI work is recorded here
  COPYRIGHT        copyright and license notices
